vel with no further object motion. This is consistent with the gripper's close command driving well past the force needed for a secure grasp of u_brown rather than stopping/backing off at contact, i.e. a genuine over-clamp of the part, not a sensor artifact or a one-tick spike (two separate monitor fires on the same clause/object both landed on this same sustained high plateau). The executor's own run_ended summary claims \"no clamp effort exceeding limits,\" which is contradicted by this evidence -- exactly the case where the executor's claim and the physical record diverge. There is no step that remediated or re-graded this: the effort returned to idle only because the part was later released, which is not a correction of the fact that u_brown was over-clamped during its pick. Because this stands as an uncorrected violation of a continuous invariant (distinct from a pose-based finding that a later reseat could cure), the run's end-state assessment is non_conformant, notwithstanding that every part's final seated pose and every other clause is clean and no bystander was disturbed.\n\nCoverage note: I relied primarily on armed monitors and get_evidence/query_snapshot for continuous workspace observation and took only a single look() at the very end of the run to visually confirm the final layout; I did not take interim looks during the run, so an unregistered-object or environmental event that left no TF/pose signature between steps could in principle have gone unseen by me, though nothing in the pose, joint, or final visual evidence suggests one occurred.","run_scope":true,"executor_terminal_status":"completed","findings_during_run":["pick_u_brown"],"recovered":false},"subscriber_count":2,"seq":55}
